{"block_id": "12c362ef6925-p0027-03", "page": 27, "bbox": [180.0800018310547, 206.45999145507812, 504.6716003417969, 232.58567810058594], "eq_number": null, "category": "prose", "gold_md": "where $a^\\mu$ is a set of four fixed numbers and $\\delta^{\\mu'}_{\\mu}$ is the four-dimensional version of the traditional Kronecker delta symbol:", "notes": ""}
{"block_id": "12c362ef6925-p0028-01", "page": 28, "bbox": [179.44158935546875, 114.0811996459961, 504.5976867675781, 162.90200805664062], "eq_number": null, "category": "prose", "gold_md": "(In matrix notation the order matters, while in index notation it is irrelevant.) We want to find the matrices $\\Lambda^{\\mu'}{}_{\\nu}$ such that the components of the matrix $\\eta_{\\mu'\\nu'}$ are the same as those of $\\eta_{\\rho\\sigma}$; that is what it means for the interval to be invariant under these transformations.", "notes": ""}
{"block_id": "12c362ef6925-p0043-02", "page": 43, "bbox": [180.09515380859375, 108.55874633789062, 504.0450439453125, 134.29563903808594], "eq_number": null, "category": "prose", "gold_md": "For a $(1,1)$ tensor $X^{\\mu}{}_{\\nu}$, the **trace** is a scalar, often denoted by leaving off the indices, which is simply the contraction:", "notes": "'trace' is bold in print"}
{"block_id": "12c362ef6925-p0045-01", "page": 45, "bbox": [179.9199981689453, 110.17798614501953, 504.7220458984375, 139.31997680664062], "eq_number": null, "category": "prose", "gold_md": "To check this, note for example that $F^{01} = \\eta^{00}\\eta^{11}F_{01}$ and $F^{12} = \\tilde{\\epsilon}^{123}B_3$. Then the first two equations in (1.93) become", "notes": ""}
{"block_id": "12c362ef6925-p0046-04", "page": 46, "bbox": [180.3699951171875, 413.845947265625, 478.1186218261719, 428.66009521484375], "eq_number": null, "category": "prose", "gold_md": "Since $d\\tau^2 = -\\eta_{\\mu\\nu}dx^\\mu dx^\\nu$, the four-velocity is automatically normalized:", "notes": ""}
{"block_id": "12c362ef6925-p0047-02", "page": 47, "bbox": [179.89999389648438, 272.177978515625, 504.9100341796875, 314.8075866699219], "eq_number": null, "category": "prose", "gold_md": "where $\\gamma = 1/\\sqrt{1 - v^2}$. For small $v$, this gives $p^0 = m + \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ (what we usually think of as rest energy plus kinetic energy) and $p^1 = mv$ (what we usually think of as Newtonian momentum). Outside this approximation, we can simply write", "notes": ""}
{"block_id": "12c362ef6925-p0063-03", "page": 63, "bbox": [174.44000244140625, 354.3675842285156, 508.70213317871094, 380.16119384765625], "eq_number": "2.1", "category": "simple", "gold_md": "$$\\mathbf{F} = m_i \\mathbf{a}.$$", "notes": "ch2 gold, transcribed from the scan crop"}
{"block_id": "12c362ef6925-p0073-08", "page": 73, "bbox": [173.90841674804688, 428.376953125, 508.5543212890625, 520.7411499023438], "eq_number": "2.8", "category": "multiline", "gold_md": "$$\\begin{aligned} y^1 &= \\phi^1(x^1, x^2, \\ldots, x^m) \\\\ y^2 &= \\phi^2(x^1, x^2, \\ldots, x^m) \\\\ &\\vdots \\\\ y^n &= \\phi^n(x^1, x^2, \\ldots, x^m). \\end{aligned}$$", "notes": "ch2 gold, transcribed from the scan crop"}
{"block_id": "12c362ef6925-p0080-04", "page": 80, "bbox": [173.55999755859375, 425.8279724121094, 508.74131774902344, 458.49176025390625], "eq_number": "2.16", "category": "calculus", "gold_md": "$$\\frac{d}{d\\lambda} = \\frac{dx^\\mu}{d\\lambda}\\partial_\\mu.$$", "notes": "ch2 gold, transcribed from the scan crop"}
{"block_id": "12c362ef6925-p0082-09", "page": 82, "bbox": [174.42999267578125, 461.4561767578125, 508.93829345703125, 504.81939697265625], "eq_number": "2.23", "category": "index", "gold_md": "$$[X, Y]^\\mu = X^\\lambda \\partial_\\lambda Y^\\mu - Y^\\lambda \\partial_\\lambda X^\\mu.$$", "notes": "ch2 gold, transcribed from the scan crop (boxed in the book)"}
{"block_id": "12c362ef6925-p0084-07", "page": 84, "bbox": [173.91000366210938, 272.74859619140625, 508.2155456542969, 333.2081604003906], "eq_number": "2.30", "category": "index_prime", "gold_md": "$$T^{\\mu'_1\\cdots\\mu'_k}{}_{\\nu'_1\\cdots\\nu'_l} = \\frac{\\partial x^{\\mu'_1}}{\\partial x^{\\mu_1}} \\cdots \\frac{\\partial x^{\\mu'_k}}{\\partial x^{\\mu_k}} \\frac{\\partial x^{\\nu_1}}{\\partial x^{\\nu'_1}} \\cdots \\frac{\\partial x^{\\nu_l}}{\\partial x^{\\nu'_l}} T^{\\mu_1\\cdots\\mu_k}{}_{\\nu_1\\cdots\\nu_l}.$$", "notes": "ch2 gold, transcribed from the scan crop"}
{"block_id": "12c362ef6925-p0085-10", "page": 85, "bbox": [173.1943359375, 461.6451721191406, 507.8501281738281, 528.3696899414062], "eq_number": "2.38", "category": "multiline", "gold_md": "$$\\begin{aligned} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x^{\\mu'}} W_{\\nu'} &= \\frac{\\partial x^\\mu}{\\partial x^{\\mu'}} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x^\\mu}\\left(\\frac{\\partial x^\\nu}{\\partial x^{\\nu'}} W_\\nu\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{\\partial x^\\mu}{\\partial x^{\\mu'}} \\frac{\\partial x^\\nu}{\\partial x^{\\nu'}}\\left(\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x^\\mu} W_\\nu\\right) + W_\\nu \\frac{\\partial x^\\mu}{\\partial x^{\\mu'}} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x^\\mu} \\frac{\\partial x^\\nu}{\\partial x^{\\nu'}}. \\end{aligned}$$", "notes": "ch2 gold, transcribed from the scan crop"}
{"block_id": "12c362ef6925-p0087-08", "page": 87, "bbox": [174.03680419921875, 394.68280029296875, 508.4516906738281, 422.09881591796875], "eq_number": "2.45", "category": "simple", "gold_md": "$$ds^2 = \\mathrm{d}\\theta^2 + \\sin^2\\theta\\, \\mathrm{d}\\phi^2.$$", "notes": "ch2 gold, transcribed from the scan crop"}
{"block_id": "12c362ef6925-p0090-04", "page": 90, "bbox": [173.7333984375, 585.7435913085938, 508.4182586669922, 613.6956787109375], "eq_number": "2.52", "category": "sign", "gold_md": "$$v = \\sqrt{1 - (U_{\\hat\\mu} V^{\\hat\\mu})^{-2}}.$$", "notes": "ch2 gold, transcribed from the scan crop"}
{"block_id": "12c362ef6925-p0092-07", "page": 92, "bbox": [173.35899353027344, 287.05999755859375, 508.5997009277344, 412.4864807128906], "eq_number": "2.59", "category": "multiline", "gold_md": "$$\\begin{aligned} \\mathrm{d}t(V) &= \\mathrm{d}t\\left(\\frac{dx^\\mu}{d\\lambda}\\partial_\\mu\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{dx^\\mu}{d\\lambda}\\mathrm{d}t(\\partial_\\mu) \\\\ &= \\frac{dx^\\mu}{d\\lambda}\\frac{\\partial t}{\\partial x^\\mu} \\\\ &= \\frac{dt}{d\\lambda}, \\end{aligned}$$", "notes": "ch2 gold, transcribed from the scan crop"}
